{"from": "artifact:pompeii-donkey-mill", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-06-millstone-drowning", "grade": "C", "note": "The Pompeian donkey-mill (mola asinaria, literally 'donkey mill'), a rotary millstone turned by animal power, in use c. 150 BCE - 300 CE and excavated in the bakeries of Pompeii: the donkey-driven millstone (mylos onikos) of the saying was a real object of the Roman world (grade C; a Roman, not Judean, find)."}
{"from": "artifact:capernaum-basalt-mills", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-06-millstone-drowning", "grade": "C", "note": "'Basalt mills for milling grain and pressing olives were also found' in the excavation of Capernaum itself - millstones were everyday objects in the very town of the Capernaum ministry (grade C; the finds are not specifically donkey-driven flour mills)."}
{"from": "text:suetonius-augustus-67", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-06-millstone-drowning", "grade": "C", "note": "Suetonius (Augustus 67): Augustus 'caused heavy weights to be tied about their necks, and had them thrown into a river' - drowning with a weight hung around the neck was an actual punishment/execution practice under Augustus (Roman practice, not Judean, so grade C)."}
{"from": "site:akeldama-tombs", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-08-09-cut-off-gehenna", "grade": "B", "note": "'Rock-cut Second Temple period burial caves known as the Akeldama tombs on the southern slope' of the Valley of Hinnom - fixes Gehenna as a real, used place in the period (reuse of the ch 05 evidence at the ch 05 grade)."}
{"from": "text:targum-jonathan-isaiah-66-24", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-08-09-cut-off-gehenna", "grade": "C", "note": "'the evildoers shall be judged in Gehenna' - the judgment association of Gehenna in Jewish exegetical tradition; the Targum's final form postdates the Second Temple period, so it evidences the tradition, not a 1st-century date (reuse of the ch 05 grade)."}
{"from": "text:songs-sabbath-sacrifice", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-10-angels-see-face", "grade": "C", "note": "The Songs 'describe worship around the throne of God in the heavenly realms... how the angels lead their prayer service in the temple on high... the inner throne room where the presence of God and the other god-like beings reside' - Second Temple Jewish texts (c. 100 BCE, copied at Qumran and Masada) imagine angels before God's face (grade C; they do not name guardian angels of individuals)."}
{"from": "observation:sheep-flock-behavior", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-12-13-lost-sheep", "grade": "C", "note": "'Sheep are flock animals and strongly gregarious' and 'sheep can become stressed when separated from their flock members' - a flock of a hundred behaves as the parable assumes, and one sheep separated from it is genuinely at risk (grade C; a biological constant, not a first-century datum)."}
{"from": "text:damascus-document-9-2-8", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-15-16-reproof-steps", "grade": "B", "note": "CD 9 (Schechter): a covenant member's charge against his neighbour must be 'proved before witnesses'; the duty to rebuke is quoted ('Thou shalt surely rebuke thy neighbour and not suffer sin upon him'), and reproof escalates stepwise - first one, then a second and a third witness before the Censor. A Second Temple community practising exactly the alone-then-witnesses escalation (grade B; the steps differ in detail from Matt 18)."}
{"from": "text:damascus-document-9-2-8", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-17-tell-the-assembly", "grade": "C", "note": "The escalation in CD 9 ends with communal judgment - 'this is the usage of the judges of the congregation' (ten men, four Levites and six Israelites) - the same structure of a matter reaching the community's judges that Matt 18:17 locates in the assembly (grade C)."}
{"from": "text:deuteronomy-19-15", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-18-15-16-reproof-steps", "note": "The two-or-three-witnesses rule of Deut 19:15, quoted in Matt 18:16 ('at the mouth of two or three witnesses every word may be established'); a biblical cross-reference, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-1-bind-loose", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-18-binding-loosing", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus (War 1.110) says the Pharisees under Queen Alexandra 'became themselves the real administrators of the public affairs... they bound and loosed [men] at their pleasure' - the bind/loose idiom applied to Pharisaic halakhic authority in exactly this period (grade C; reuse of the ch 16 evidence)."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-avot-3-2", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-19-20-two-or-three", "grade": "C", "note": "Avot 3:2: 'if two sit together and there are words of Torah [spoken] between them, then the Shekhinah abides among them' - the divine presence resting on a gathering of two, the same arithmetic as 'where two or three are gathered' (grade C; Mishnah c. 200 CE preserves Second Temple-era teaching)."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-avot-3-6", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-19-20-two-or-three", "grade": "C", "note": "Avot 3:6: 'when ten sit together and occupy themselves with Torah, the Shechinah abides among them... even of three... even of two' - graded presence with decreasing numbers down to two (grade C). Redacted c. 200 CE, so it may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly."}
{"from": "text:genesis-4-24", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-18-21-22-seventy-seven", "note": "Lamech's seventy-sevenfold vengeance (Gen 4:24) inverted into seventy-times-seven forgiveness; a biblical cross-reference, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-320-tribute", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-23-24-ten-thousand-talents", "grade": "C", "note": "Ant. 17.318-320: Perea and Galilee paid Antipas tribute 'annually to two hundred talents', Philip's realm one hundred, Archelaus's six hundred - the talent is the unit of royal accounts, and against a tribute of 100-600 talents a year, a debt of ten thousand talents is an impossible, hyperbolic sum (grade C)."}
{"from": "artifact:babatha-archive", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-18-30-prison-until-payment", "grade": "C", "note": "The archive documents debts and legal contracts among Judean Jews (96-134 CE); it shows debt handled by contract and courts, the world in which a creditor's remedies (including distraint) operate, and does not itself evidence imprisonment for debt (reuse of the ch 05 grade)."}
